## Repo map

```
{{PYTHON_PACKAGE}}/
  __init__.py                      NautobotAppConfig subclass
  models.py                        App data models
  views.py  tables.py  filters.py  forms.py  urls.py
  navigation.py                    Nav menu items
  jobs/                            Nautobot Jobs
  api/                             serializers.py, views.py, urls.py
  migrations/
  tests/
.github/workflows/claude.yml       The Lane B driver
.github/scripts/journal-entry.sh   Metrics-append script the workflow calls
docs/journal.md                    Per-issue build metrics, appended by the workflow only — never edit by hand
pyproject.toml                     Poetry project definition and dependencies
.pre-commit-config.yaml            black/ruff pre-commit hooks
.env                                Local secrets — never commit, see .env.example
```

Touch only files named in the issue or listed above. In particular, do not
create `admin.py` — Nautobot surfaces models through its own UI (views,
tables, filters, navigation), not Django admin; an admin registration would
sit unused.

## Conventions

- **Never edit `docs/journal.md` by hand or from within a PR branch.** It's appended by `.github/workflows/claude.yml` *after* your PR merges, via `.github/scripts/journal-entry.sh`. Editing it in your branch means every open PR touches the same file and goes conflicting the moment any other PR merges.
- Follow Nautobot App conventions: subclass Nautobot's base model/view/filter classes rather than plain Django ones where a Nautobot equivalent exists.
- Verify Nautobot APIs against the installed package rather than assuming them — the workflow's Poetry step installs a real Nautobot into the runner specifically so imports, exports, and migration names can be checked locally instead of guessed, e.g.:

  ```
  poetry run python -c "import nautobot.apps.models as m; print(dir(m))"
  ```
- Model changes ship with their migration (`poetry run nautobot-server makemigrations`), committed alongside the model change.
- Keep commit and PR scope to the files named in the issue or in the repo map above.
- {{ADDITIONAL_CONVENTIONS}}

## Definition of done

- The issue's acceptance criteria are met.
- Tests pass: `poetry run nautobot-server test {{PYTHON_PACKAGE}}`.
- Lint is clean: `poetry run ruff check .` and `poetry run black --check .`.
- Any shell script added is `bash -n`-clean and executable (`chmod +x`).
- Any JSON added is valid (`jq . <file>` succeeds).
- PR description explains what you implemented, what you verified, and anything you could not verify unattended.
